# Prototype

{{PREAMBLE}}

## Overview

`/prototype` is the Factory's UI-prototype lane. `/plan-design` produces the **design record**
(`02a-plan-design.md`): tokens-as-code, a complete screen inventory, an every-edge navigation
graph, and stacked Mermaid screen-flows. What it does not produce is something the operator can
*look at* — you cannot see the screens until the build loop implements them. `/prototype` closes
that gap: it renders the design record into a **clickable, high-fidelity HTML prototype** the
operator reviews **before** any code is written, on the same engine (run/artifact/gate harness,
redaction, memory) as every other lane.

It is **on-demand**: run it when a UI product wants a visual proof of the design before build. It
is a *wrapper* — it composes `frontend-design` (intentional visual direction) and
`visualization-expert` (any data-viz), and uses the `browse` tool to self-verify the rendered
result. On top of them it adds the Factory's fidelity discipline: the prototype is styled from the
design record's emitted tokens **verbatim**, and a coverage gate (`lib/prototype-plan.ts`) proves
every screen has a page, every navigation edge resolves, and no page invents a token.

## When to Activate

Activate when:
- `/plan-design` has written a design record (`02a-plan-design.md`) for a component with a UI
  (`framework: react`/`flutter`), and the operator wants to *see* the screens as a clickable
  prototype before the build loop implements them.
- The user asks to "prototype the UI", "make a clickable mockup", "show me the screens", or "render
  the design so I can click through it".

**Do not activate** (adjacent skills own this):
- `plan-design` — owns the *specification* (the design record the build implements verbatim);
  `/prototype` renders a *proof* of that spec, it never writes or revises the spec. If the design
  is wrong, fix it in `/plan-design` and re-run `/prototype`.
- `frontend-design` / `visualization-expert` — the craft skills this wrapper composes; it
  orchestrates and fidelity-checks them, it doesn't replace them.
- The build loop (Implementer + `/review`) — owns *implementing* the design as real app code;
  `/prototype` produces a throwaway visual proof, not shippable UI.
- `discover` / `plan-arch` / `plan-delivery` — own the PRD, the stack, and the increment backlog;
  `/prototype` is a design-fidelity step, not a planning one, and gates nothing downstream.

## Core Concepts

- **The prototype is the artifact, and it renders the design — it does not redesign.** A prototype
  that invents colours, spacing, or type the design record never set is the failure mode — the
  design-fidelity analogue of `/plan-design`'s AI-slop gate. Fidelity is *derived*: the token files
  give real visual accuracy, not an approximation.
- **One clickable HTML page per screen.** The design record's screen inventory maps one-to-one to
  self-contained HTML pages; an `index.html` gallery and a device frame tie them together. The
  every-edge navigation graph maps directly to working in-prototype `<a href>` links, so the
  operator clicks through real routes.
- **Self-contained and offline.** The prototype ships zero external dependencies — tokens inlined,
  no CDN, no network. It renders in the `browse` tool with the network off, and is diffable as a
  run artifact. That is why HTML wins over Figma (paid, external, not generable) or a wireframe
  tool (low fidelity).
- **Coverage is a gate, not a garnish.** Before hand-off, `lib/prototype-plan.ts` proves, offline:
  every screen has a page (`missing-page`), every navigation edge resolves to a working link that
  lands on an existing page (`dangling-link`), and no page uses a token absent from the record
  (`invented-token`). A gap fails the lane.
- **On-demand, gates nothing.** Unlike `/plan-delivery`'s PLAN → BUILD sign-off, `/prototype` is
  optional and re-run on demand when the design changes. It is the higher-fidelity sibling of the
  screen-flow diagram, one step downstream of `/plan-design`.

## Workflow

Freedom level: **medium** — the fidelity rule and the coverage gate are fixed; the render is yours.

1. **Read the design record.** Load `02a-plan-design.md`: the screen inventory (every screen), the
   navigation graph (every `screen → action → screen` edge), and the design tokens emitted as code
   (colour / space / type / radius). If there is no design record, stop and hand back to
   `/plan-design`.
2. **Inline the tokens verbatim (`frontend-design`).** Turn the record's emitted tokens into a
   single self-contained stylesheet (CSS custom properties inlined, no CDN). Use *only* the tokens
   the record defines — do not invent a colour or a spacing step. Any data-viz uses
   `visualization-expert` and the same tokens.
3. **Render one page per screen.** For each screen in the inventory, emit one self-contained HTML
   page styled from the tokens, plus an `index.html` gallery and a device frame. A screen with no
   page is a coverage failure.
4. **Wire the navigation graph.** For each `from → action → to` edge, add a working in-prototype
   `<a href>` on the `from` page that lands on the `to` page. Every edge must resolve; a dangling
   route is a coverage failure.
5. **Self-verify with `browse`, offline.** Open `index.html` and click through the wired routes
   with the network off — prove it renders self-contained with zero external dependencies.
6. **Run the coverage gate.** Emit the prototype manifest (screens + nav + tokens from the record;
   pages + their links + tokens-used from what you generated) and verify it with
   `lib/prototype-plan.ts`: screen-coverage, link-coverage, and token-fidelity must all pass. Fix
   any finding in the render, never by loosening the record.
7. **Record the run artifact.** The prototype follows `/plan-design` in step 2, so it is a **branch
   artifact** with a sub-sequence seq — `2b`, recording the manifest as `02b-prototype.md` beside
   the `02b-prototype/` HTML directory:
   ```bash
   fac run artifact --seq 2b --step prototype \
     --inputs .factory/runs/$RUN/02a-plan-design.md --body-file prototype-manifest.md
   ```
   It records `02a-plan-design.md` as its input, so a design change re-opens the prototype
   (make-like cascade).
8. **Hand off.** Show the operator the clickable prototype for sign-off. If they approve, the design
   proceeds to `/plan-delivery` + the build loop; if not, the change goes back to `/plan-design`.

## Practical Guidance

- Render the tokens, don't reinterpret them — the whole point is a faithful proof, so a swatch the
  operator sees must be the exact token the build will implement.
- Keep every page self-contained: inline the CSS, no external fonts or CDNs — the offline render is
  a hard exit criterion, not a nicety.
- Wire every edge the navigation graph lists, including back/dismiss transitions; a prototype you
  can't click all the way through isn't a proof.
- If a screen or edge feels missing, the fix is in `/plan-design` (the spec), not an invented page
  here — hand it back.
- Treat the prototype as throwaway: it proves the design, it is not the start of the build.

## Examples

**Example:**
```
Input:  02a-plan-design.md (Repair Tracker web) — 5 screens (onboarding, repair-list, repair-detail,
        assign-sheet, empty-state), a navigation graph of 6 edges, tokens as CSS custom properties.
Output: 02b-prototype/ — index.html gallery + a device frame + 5 self-contained HTML pages styled
        from the tokens verbatim, wired by the 6 navigation edges (every <a href> resolves).
        Coverage gate (lib/prototype-plan.ts): 5/5 screens covered, 6/6 edges resolved, 0 invented
        tokens → pass. Self-verified in browse with the network off.
        run artifact 02b-prototype.md (the manifest) records 02a-plan-design.md as its input.
Handoff → operator sign-off → /plan-delivery + build loop (or back to /plan-design on a change).
```

## Guidelines

1. Render the design record; never redesign it — invented tokens fail the fidelity gate.
2. One self-contained HTML page per screen in the inventory; a missing page fails coverage.
3. Wire every navigation edge as a working in-prototype link; a dangling route fails coverage.
4. Ship zero external dependencies; prove the offline render in `browse`.
5. Record the manifest as `02b-prototype.md` with `02a-plan-design.md` as its input.
6. Hand the clickable prototype to the operator for sign-off; changes go back to `/plan-design`.

## Gotchas

1. **Invented fidelity**: a colour/spacing/type the record never set is the primary failure mode —
   the token-fidelity gate catches it.
2. **A missing screen**: skipping an inventory screen (or an empty/error/permission route) fails
   screen-coverage — render them all.
3. **A dangling link**: a navigation edge that lands nowhere (or a page that never wires the link)
   fails link-coverage — wire every edge.
4. **External dependencies**: a CDN font or script breaks the offline render — inline everything.
5. **Redesigning here**: fixing a design problem by inventing a page/token instead of handing it
   back to `/plan-design` corrupts the spec-vs-proof boundary.

## Integration

- `plan-design` — writes the design record (`02a-plan-design.md`) `/prototype` renders; a design
  change re-opens the prototype.
- `frontend-design` (craft) — supplies the intentional visual direction the render honours verbatim.
- `visualization-expert` (craft) — supplies any data-viz, styled from the same tokens.
- `browse` (tool) — renders and clicks through the prototype to self-verify it, offline.
- `plan-delivery` — runs after operator sign-off to decompose the approved design into increments.
- Run harness (`fac run`) — records the manifest as a sub-sequenced `02b-prototype.md`; the lane
  gates nothing downstream (it is an on-demand proof).
- Verifier — `lib/prototype-plan.ts` proves the prototype covers the design record (screen-coverage,
  link-coverage, token-fidelity).

